# Troubleshooting DNS over TLS (DoT) SSL/TLS Errors in Unbound

When you configure Unbound to serve DNS over TLS (DoT) using a Let's Encrypt certificate, you will often see SSL handshake failures or "Permission denied" errors on the first start (or after a reload).  

These errors are almost always caused by one of two things:

1. The `unbound` user cannot read the certificate/private key files because of restrictive permissions on the Let's Encrypt directories.
2. **AppArmor** (common on Debian, Ubuntu, and derivatives) is blocking access even when filesystem permissions look correct.

This playbook walks through diagnosis and the recommended fixes in order of preference.

## 1. Diagnose the Error

Check the Unbound logs:

```bash
sudo journalctl -u unbound -n 100 --no-pager
# Live tail
sudo journalctl -u unbound -f
Common error patterns:

Permission denied when loading tls-service-key or tls-service-pem
ssl handshake failed
error: unable to load certificate chain file
AppArmor denial messages in syslog or dmesg

Also check for AppArmor denials:
Bashsudo dmesg | grep -i apparmor | tail -20
sudo grep -E 'unbound.*denied|apparmor' /var/log/syslog | tail -20
2. Check Filesystem Permissions
Let's Encrypt stores certificates in restrictive locations:
Bashls -la /etc/letsencrypt/live/yourdomain.example.com/
ls -la /etc/letsencrypt/archive/yourdomain.example.com/
These directories are normally owned by root with very tight permissions. The unbound user (or the process before it drops privileges) cannot read privkey.pem or fullchain.pem.
3. Recommended Fix: Extend the AppArmor Profile (Best Long-Term Solution)
On Debian/Ubuntu-based systems the default AppArmor profile for Unbound only allows access to /etc/unbound/** and a few other paths. It explicitly blocks /etc/letsencrypt/.
Steps

Create a local override (this file is preserved across package updates):Bashsudo mkdir -p /etc/apparmor.d/local

sudo tee /etc/apparmor.d/local/usr.sbin.unbound > /dev/null << 'EOF'
# Allow Unbound to read Let's Encrypt certificates for DoT / DoH
/etc/letsencrypt/archive/** r,
/etc/letsencrypt/live/** r,
EOF
Reload the profile:Bashsudo apparmor_parser --replace /etc/apparmor.d/usr.sbin.unboundAlternative (interactive):
If you have recent denials in the logs, run:Bashsudo aa-logprofand follow the prompts to allow the observed accesses for Unbound.
Verify and restart:Bashsudo aa-status | grep -E 'unbound|letsencrypt'
sudo systemctl restart unbound

Advantages of this method:

No changes to certificate ownership or permissions.
Certbot renewals continue to work with zero extra configuration.
Least privilege (only read access is granted via mandatory access control).

4. Alternative: Copy Certificates into /etc/unbound/ (Quick & Simple)
If you prefer to avoid editing AppArmor (or are on a non-AppArmor system), copy the certificates into Unbound’s own directory, which is already permitted by the default profile.
Bashsudo mkdir -p /etc/unbound/certs

sudo cp /etc/letsencrypt/live/yourdomain.example.com/fullchain.pem /etc/unbound/certs/
sudo cp /etc/letsencrypt/live/yourdomain.example.com/privkey.pem /etc/unbound/certs/

sudo chown -R unbound:unbound /etc/unbound/certs
sudo chmod 640 /etc/unbound/certs/*.pem
Then update your Unbound config to point at these paths (see section 6).
For certificate renewals, create a deploy hook so the copies are updated automatically:
Bashsudo tee /etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/unbound-cert-copy.sh > /dev/null << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
cp /etc/letsencrypt/live/yourdomain.example.com/fullchain.pem /etc/unbound/certs/
cp /etc/letsencrypt/live/yourdomain.example.com/privkey.pem /etc/unbound/certs/
chown unbound:unbound /etc/unbound/certs/*.pem
chmod 640 /etc/unbound/certs/*.pem
systemctl reload unbound
EOF

sudo chmod +x /etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/unbound-cert-copy.sh
5. (Avoid if Possible) Loosen Permissions on Let's Encrypt Directories
Only use this method if the previous two are not viable. It carries higher risk because it makes the private key readable by more processes.
Example:
Bashsudo chgrp -R unbound /etc/letsencrypt/live/yourdomain.example.com \
                      /etc/letsencrypt/archive/yourdomain.example.com

sudo chmod -R g+rx /etc/letsencrypt/live/yourdomain.example.com \
                   /etc/letsencrypt/archive/yourdomain.example.com

sudo chmod g+r /etc/letsencrypt/archive/yourdomain.example.com/*.pem
Then restart Unbound and monitor closely.
Security warning: This approach is less safe than the AppArmor or copy methods. Revert it once you have a better solution in place.
6. Unbound DoT Configuration Example
unboundserver:
    # ... your existing settings ...

    # DoT listener
    interface: 0.0.0.0@853
    interface: ::0@853
    tls-port: 853

    # Certificate for serving DoT (use fullchain.pem)
    tls-service-key: "/etc/unbound/certs/privkey.pem"     # or /etc/letsencrypt/... path
    tls-service-pem:  "/etc/unbound/certs/fullchain.pem"

    # Optional hardening
    tls-use-sni: yes
Validate and apply:
Bashsudo unbound-checkconf /etc/unbound/unbound.conf
sudo systemctl restart unbound
Test:
Bashkdig @your-server-ip -p 853 +tls yourdomain.example.com
# or
openssl s_client -connect your-server-ip:853 -servername yourdomain.example.com
7. Additional Tips

Certbot dry-run test after any changes: sudo certbot renew --dry-run
SELinux systems (RHEL/Rocky/Alma/Fedora): Use audit2allow, semanage fcontext, and restorecon instead of AppArmor steps.
Temporary verbosity: Add verbosity: 2 (or 3) under server: while troubleshooting, then reduce it.
Firewall: Allow TCP/853 if you intend to expose DoT externally (or restrict to trusted networks/VPN).
Monitoring: Watch logs around renewal time (certs are valid for 90 days).
